 *                           // ]
 *  }, cancelTimeMs + t + 15)    
 */



🔑 CORE JAVASCRIPT CONCEPTS YOU LEARN FROM THIS CODE
1️⃣ Higher-Order Functions (Functions Returning Functions)

📌 Concept:

A function that returns another function

📌 In your code:

return function cancelFn() {
    clearInterval(timerId);
};


➡️ cancellable() returns a new function (cancelFn)
➡️ That returned function can be called later

🧠 Interview thought:

“Functions in JavaScript are first-class citizens.”

2️⃣ Closures (VERY IMPORTANT 🔥)

📌 Concept:

A function remembers variables from where it was created

📌 In your code:

const timerId = setInterval(...)

return function cancelFn(){
    clearInterval(timerId);
};


➡️ cancelFn still knows what timerId is
➡️ Even after cancellable() has finished executing

🧠 This is closure

📢 Interview one-liner:

“The cancel function closes over timerId and uses it later.”

3️⃣ Timers & Asynchronous Execution

📌 Concepts used:

setInterval()

clearInterval()

📌 In your code:

setInterval(() => {
    fn(...args);
}, t);


➡️ Runs fn every t milliseconds
➡️ Happens asynchronously

4️⃣ Immediate Execution + Scheduled Execution

📌 Concept:

Run something now

Then run it repeatedly later

📌 In your code:

fn(...args);          // runs immediately
setInterval(...);     // runs repeatedly


🧠 Why this matters:

Matches real-world needs (polling, live updates, logging)

5️⃣ Spread Operator (...args)

📌 Concept:

Expands an array into arguments

📌 In your code:

fn(...args);


If:

args = [4]


Then:

fn(...args)  → fn(4)

6️⃣ Controlled Side Effects

📌 Concept:

Code causes effects (logs, updates, network calls)

But gives control to stop it

📌 In your code:

const cancel = cancellable(...)
setTimeout(cancel, cancelTimeMs);


➡️ Side effect stops exactly when needed

7️⃣ Time-based Program Flow

📌 Concept:

Program behavior depends on time

📌 You learn:

Delayed execution

Repeated execution

Cancellation

This is real-world JavaScript (UI, APIs, animations, polling).

8️⃣ Function Composition with Parameters

📌 Concept:

Passing a function as data

📌 In your code:

cancellable(log, args, t)


➡️ log is passed without calling it
➡️ JavaScript executes it later

🔄 FULL FLOW IN SIMPLE WORDS

Call cancellable(fn, args, t)

fn runs immediately

fn starts running every t milliseconds

cancellable returns a cancel function

Calling cancel() stops future executions

